INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: hard / L4 — Inactive market triggers Level 3 reclassification
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Meridian Capital holds a portfolio of mortgage-backed securities previously classified as Level 2 under ASC 820. During Q4 2025, trading volume drops 90%, bid-ask spreads triple, and broker quotes become indicative rather than executable. Meridian begins relying on its internal cash flow model with management-developed prepayment and default assumptions. How should Meridian account for this change?', '["Transfer the securities to Level 3 and disclose the reason, amounts, and total gains or losses for the transfer", "Retain the securities at Level 2 because the original observable inputs were Level 2 at inception", "Reclassify the securities as Level 1 once broker quotes become available again", "Disclose the market disruption in the notes but continue measuring at Level 2 with the same inputs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 820-10-35-54 requires classification based on the lowest level of significant input currently used. When observable inputs become unavailable and management substitutes unobservable assumptions, the measurement transfers to Level 3, with required disclosure of amounts and reasons. Retaining at Level 2 is incorrect because classification follows current inputs, not historical ones.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Matrix pricing as Level 2 market approach technique
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Brookfield Financial values a municipal bond using benchmark AAA-rated municipal yields adjusted for the bond''s credit quality, maturity, and sector. No quoted price for the identical bond exists, but the benchmark yields are observable. Under ASC 820, what is this valuation technique called, and at what level is it classified?', '["Income approach; Level 3", "Matrix pricing, a form of the market approach; Level 2", "Cost approach; Level 2", "Discounted cash flow technique; Level 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-35-36 describes matrix pricing as a market approach technique relying on a security''s relationship to benchmark quoted prices rather than quoted prices for the identical instrument. Because benchmark yields are observable and adjustments are based on observable factors, the measurement is Level 2. The income approach converts future cash flows to present value, which is a different technique.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Fair value option irrevocability
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 825, once an entity elects the fair value option for a financial instrument, what is the nature of that election?', '["Revocable at each reporting date at management''s discretion", "Revocable only at the beginning of each fiscal year", "Irrevocable once made", "Revocable with auditor approval"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 825-10-25-4 specifies that the fair value option election is irrevocable and made on an instrument-by-instrument basis at initial recognition or upon a qualifying event. Once elected, the entity cannot subsequently choose to measure the instrument at amortized cost. Allowing annual revocation is incorrect because ASC 825 provides no mechanism for unwinding the election.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Market-corroborated inputs as Level 2
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Hawthorne Corp. values an interest rate swap using a model where inputs are derived principally from observable market data through correlation with benchmark yield curves. Under ASC 820, these inputs are best described as:', '["Level 1 inputs because they originate from market data", "Level 3 inputs because they are model-derived", "Unobservable inputs because they require a correlation model", "Market-corroborated inputs, classified as Level 2"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 820-10-35-48 defines market-corroborated inputs as those derived principally from or corroborated by observable market data through correlation or other means. These qualify as Level 2 because they are based on observable data, even though a model is required. They are not Level 1, which requires unadjusted quoted prices for identical instruments in active markets.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Fair value option: upfront issuance costs expensed immediately
(2, 'Fair Value', 'On January 1, 2026, First National Bank issues a $10 million note payable and elects the fair value option under ASC 825. Issuance costs total $150,000. At December 31, 2026, the note''s fair value is $9,800,000. How should First National account for the issuance costs and the year-end fair value change?', '["Expense the $150,000 issuance costs immediately; recognize a $200,000 gain from the decrease in the liability''s fair value", "Defer the $150,000 issuance costs and amortize over the note''s life; recognize a $200,000 gain", "Capitalize the $150,000 as a discount on the note; recognize a $50,000 gain net of amortized issuance costs", "Expense the $150,000 immediately; recognize a $200,000 unrealized loss in OCI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 825-10-25-3 requires that when the fair value option is elected, upfront costs and fees are recognized in earnings immediately — not deferred or capitalized. The decrease from $10,000,000 to $9,800,000 reduces the liability by $200,000, recognized as a gain in net income. Deferring issuance costs is incorrect because the fair value option eliminates the ability to capitalize transaction costs.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L3 — Multi-period excess earnings method classification
(2, 'Fair Value', 'AcquireCo uses the multi-period excess earnings method (MPEEM) to value a customer relationship intangible acquired in a business combination. Projected cash flows are $600,000 in Year 1, declining 15% annually over 5 years, with a 14% discount rate. The MPEEM isolates cash flows by deducting contributory asset charges. Under ASC 820, how is this technique classified?', '["Market approach because it uses comparable intangible transactions", "Income approach using a technique that isolates the intangible''s specific cash flow contribution", "Cost approach because it measures the cost to replace the customer relationship", "A hybrid approach classified outside the three standard valuation approaches"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-35-32 classifies the multi-period excess earnings method as an income approach technique because it converts expected future cash flows into a single present value. The MPEEM isolates cash flows attributable to a single intangible by subtracting returns on contributory assets. The market approach is incorrect because MPEEM relies on projected cash flows, not comparable transaction prices.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Market participants characteristics
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 820, which of the following describes the characteristics of market participants?', '["Related parties who regularly transact with the reporting entity", "Any willing buyer or seller regardless of knowledge", "Knowledgeable, willing, able to transact, and independent of the reporting entity", "Only institutional investors with significant market share"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 820-10-20 defines market participants as buyers and sellers in the principal or most advantageous market who are independent, knowledgeable, able to transact, and willing but not compelled. Related parties fail the independence criterion and do not qualify as market participants under the standard.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Goodwill impairment as nonrecurring fair value measurement
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Dalton Industries determines its reporting unit''s carrying amount exceeds fair value by $2.4 million during goodwill impairment testing under ASC 350. The goodwill balance is $5 million. Under ASC 820, how is this fair value measurement classified?', '["Recurring, because goodwill is tested at least annually", "Nonrecurring, because the impairment loss is a one-time event that never recurs", "Neither recurring nor nonrecurring — impairment tests are outside ASC 820 scope", "Nonrecurring, because goodwill is not remeasured at fair value each reporting period but only when impairment is indicated"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 820-10-50-1 classifies goodwill impairment as nonrecurring because goodwill is not remeasured at fair value every period — fair value is determined only when impairment testing is triggered. The annual test is a test for impairment, not a periodic remeasurement to fair value; goodwill remains at carrying amount unless impaired.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Observable yield curves as Level 2 inputs
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Prescott Financial values a plain-vanilla interest rate swap using the SOFR yield curve, derived from active exchange-traded futures contracts and publicly available rate data. Under ASC 820, how are these yield curve inputs classified within the fair value hierarchy?', '["Level 2, because they are observable market data other than quoted prices for identical instruments", "Level 1, because they are derived from active exchange-traded contracts", "Level 3, because a model is required to construct the yield curve", "Not classifiable until the entire valuation model is evaluated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 820-10-35-47 identifies interest rates and yield curves observable at commonly quoted intervals as Level 2 inputs. Although these rates originate from exchange-traded instruments, the yield curve itself is not a quoted price for the identical asset, so it does not qualify as Level 1. Level 1 requires unadjusted quoted prices for the identical instrument.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Level 3 roll-forward reconciliation disclosure
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Crestview Holdings reports $45 million in recurring Level 3 fair value measurements. Its auditor requests a reconciliation of beginning to ending balances showing purchases, sales, issuances, settlements, gains and losses in earnings and OCI, and transfers in and out of Level 3. Under ASC 820, is this reconciliation required?', '["No — roll-forward reconciliations are only required for Level 1 measurements", "Yes — a roll-forward reconciliation is required for all recurring Level 3 fair value measurements", "Only if total Level 3 measurements exceed 10% of total assets", "Only if the entity is a public company subject to SEC reporting requirements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-50-2(c) requires a reconciliation of beginning and ending balances for all recurring Level 3 fair value measurements, including gains/losses in earnings and OCI, purchases, sales, issuances, settlements, and transfers into/out of Level 3. This applies without a materiality threshold. Level 1 measurements require the least — not the most — disclosure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Monte Carlo simulation classified as income approach
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Garland Corp. values a contingent consideration obligation arising from a business combination using a Monte Carlo simulation that models thousands of revenue outcome scenarios and discounts the expected payout. Under ASC 820, this valuation technique is classified as:', '["Market approach because it simulates market conditions", "Cost approach because it estimates the economic cost of the obligation", "Income approach because it converts expected future amounts to a present value", "A statistical approach outside the three ASC 820 valuation techniques"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 820-10-35-32 classifies option pricing models — including Monte Carlo simulations and binomial lattice models — as income approach techniques because they convert expected future cash flows or outcomes into a single present value. The market approach uses prices from actual market transactions involving comparable items, not simulated outcomes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Measurement date concept
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 820, fair value is measured as of what point in time?', '["The average of quarter-end dates during the fiscal year", "The date the asset was originally acquired", "The date of the most recent independent appraisal", "The measurement date, typically the reporting date (balance sheet date)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 820-10-05-1B specifies that fair value is measured at a specific point in time — the measurement date, typically the balance sheet date. Fair value is not an average over a period or tied to the original acquisition date. The acquisition date is incorrect because fair value reflects current exit price conditions, not historical transaction prices.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Transfers between Level 1 and Level 2 disclosure
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Redstone Corp. holds corporate bonds previously measured using unadjusted quoted prices on an active exchange (Level 1). During the period, the exchange delists the bonds, and Redstone now values them using quoted prices for similar bonds in active markets. Under ASC 820, what must Redstone disclose?', '["The transfer from Level 1 to Level 2, the amounts, and the reasons for the transfer", "Nothing — transfers between Level 1 and Level 2 do not require disclosure", "Only the fair value at the end of the period without referencing the level change", "A restatement of all prior periods reflecting the bonds at Level 2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 820-10-50-2(b) requires disclosure of transfers between Level 1 and Level 2, including amounts and reasons. This transparency helps financial statement users understand changes in measurement reliability. ASC 820 explicitly requires disclosure of transfers between all hierarchy levels, including between Level 1 and Level 2.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Defensive use of nonfinancial asset vs highest and best use
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Northfield Pharma acquires a competitor''s patent solely to prevent the competitor from marketing a rival drug. Northfield will not license or commercialize the patent. An appraiser determines the patent''s value-in-use to Northfield is $0 (defensive only), but market participants would value it at $8.5 million if licensed. Under ASC 820, what fair value should Northfield recognize at the acquisition date?', '["$0, reflecting Northfield''s intended defensive use", "$8.5 million, because ASC 820 measures fair value based on market participant assumptions, not entity-specific use", "The midpoint of $0 and $8.5 million ($4.25 million) to reflect both perspectives", "$8.5 million less a marketability discount for the lack of an active patent market"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-35-10B requires that nonfinancial asset fair value assume the highest and best use by market participants, even if the entity uses the asset differently. Defensive use is entity-specific intent, not a market participant assumption, so the $8.5 million reflects how market participants would deploy the patent. Measuring at $0 is incorrect because ASC 820 prohibits basing nonfinancial asset fair value solely on entity-specific intent.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Physical deterioration and functional obsolescence in cost approach
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Hemlock Manufacturing uses the cost approach to value a specialized furnace. Current replacement cost of a new furnace is $1,200,000. The appraiser identifies $180,000 of physical deterioration and $120,000 of functional obsolescence from lower energy efficiency compared to current models. Under ASC 820, what is the fair value using the cost approach?', '["$1,200,000", "$1,020,000", "$900,000", "$1,080,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 820-10-35-34 describes the cost approach as replacement cost adjusted for physical deterioration, functional obsolescence, and economic obsolescence. Fair value = $1,200,000 minus $180,000 minus $120,000 = $900,000. The $1,080,000 answer deducts only physical deterioration and ignores functional obsolescence, but both adjustments must be applied under the cost approach.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — VIE exclusion from fair value option
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Summit Financial consolidates a variable interest entity (VIE) under ASC 810. The CFO proposes electing the fair value option under ASC 825 for Summit''s interest in the VIE to simplify reporting. Under ASC 825, is this election permitted?', '["Yes — the fair value option is available for all financial instruments without exception", "Yes, but only if the VIE holds primarily financial assets", "No — VIE interests require regulatory approval for fair value treatment", "No — interests in consolidated VIEs are specifically excluded from the fair value option"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 825-10-15-5 explicitly excludes interests in variable interest entities that the entity is required to consolidate from the fair value option. This ensures the consolidation model under ASC 810 is not undermined by an entity-level election. ASC 825 identifies several exclusions, including consolidated VIEs, pension obligations, and consolidated subsidiaries.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Derivatives within ASC 820 scope
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 820, are derivative instruments within the scope of the fair value measurement framework?', '["No — derivatives are measured under ASC 815 exclusively", "Only if the derivative is exchange-traded", "Yes — derivatives measured at fair value under ASC 815 apply ASC 820''s measurement framework", "Only if the derivative qualifies for hedge accounting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 820-10-15-1A confirms that ASC 820 applies to all fair value measurements required or permitted by other GAAP topics, including derivatives under ASC 815. ASC 815 requires derivatives to be measured at fair value, and ASC 820 provides the measurement framework. ASC 815 determines that derivatives use fair value; ASC 820 defines how to measure it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Available-for-sale debt securities as recurring measurement
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Willow Creek Bank holds available-for-sale (AFS) debt securities measured at fair value through OCI each quarter. No impairment events occurred during 2025. Under ASC 820, how are these fair value measurements classified?', '["Nonrecurring, because impairment was not triggered during the period", "Recurring, because AFS debt securities are remeasured at fair value each reporting period", "Not subject to ASC 820 classification because they are measured through OCI", "Recurring only in years when a credit loss is recognized"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-50-1 defines recurring fair value measurements as those required each reporting period. Available-for-sale debt securities under ASC 320 are remeasured to fair value every quarter with unrealized gains and losses in OCI, making them recurring regardless of impairment. The nonrecurring classification confuses the impairment test with the periodic remeasurement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Fair value option: instrument-by-instrument election granularity
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 825, at what level of granularity is the fair value option election made?', '["At the portfolio level for all similar financial instruments", "At the entity level for all financial assets or all financial liabilities", "On an instrument-by-instrument basis", "At the reporting segment level"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 825-10-25-4 requires the fair value option election on an instrument-by-instrument basis, not at the portfolio, entity, or segment level. This allows selective application while maintaining comparability. Portfolio-level elections could allow entities to selectively measure instruments at fair value only when beneficial, undermining the standard''s design.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Business combination acquisition date FV as nonrecurring
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Apex Corp. acquires Zenith Inc. under ASC 805 and measures Zenith''s identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value totaling $42 million on the acquisition date. In subsequent periods, the acquired items are carried at their post-acquisition measurement basis. Under ASC 820, the acquisition date fair value measurement is classified as:', '["Recurring, because ASC 805 requires ongoing fair value monitoring of acquired assets", "Neither recurring nor nonrecurring — business combinations are outside ASC 820 scope", "Recurring if the acquired assets include any financial instruments", "Nonrecurring, because the assets and liabilities are measured at fair value only at the acquisition date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 820-10-50-1 classifies business combination measurements as nonrecurring because assets acquired and liabilities assumed are measured at fair value only at the acquisition date. After acquisition, those items follow their respective post-acquisition measurement guidance. ASC 805 does not require ongoing fair value remeasurement of acquired net assets.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Deposit liabilities exclusion from fair value option
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Riverdale Savings Bank considers electing the fair value option under ASC 825 for its customer deposit liabilities — checking and savings accounts. Under ASC 825, are deposit liabilities eligible for the fair value option?', '["No — deposit liabilities of depository institutions are specifically excluded from the fair value option", "Yes — all bank liabilities qualify for the fair value option", "Yes, but only for time deposits with maturities exceeding one year", "Yes, provided the bank obtains regulatory approval from the FDIC"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 825-10-15-5 explicitly excludes deposit liabilities of depository institutions from the fair value option. This recognizes that deposit liabilities have unique characteristics such as FDIC insurance and demand features. Allowing the election for all bank liabilities is incorrect because ASC 825 specifically carves out deposit liabilities along with pension obligations and consolidated subsidiary interests.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Sensitivity analysis required only for Level 3
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 820, a narrative sensitivity analysis describing how changes in unobservable inputs would affect the fair value is specifically required for which hierarchy level?', '["Level 1 measurements only", "Level 3 measurements", "All three levels equally", "Level 2 measurements only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-50-2(g) requires a sensitivity analysis describing how changes in significant unobservable inputs would affect Level 3 fair value measurements. This disclosure is not required for Level 1 or Level 2, which use observable inputs. Requiring sensitivity analysis for all levels equally is incorrect because it targets Level 3 due to the inherent estimation uncertainty.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Comparable company multiples as market approach
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Evergreen Capital values a private company using price-to-earnings multiples derived from five publicly traded peer companies, adjusted for size and liquidity differences. Under ASC 820, this valuation technique is best described as:', '["Income approach using a capitalization rate", "Cost approach based on the company''s invested capital", "Market approach using comparable company analysis", "A blended approach requiring Level 1 classification"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 820-10-35-36 describes comparable company analysis as a market approach technique using pricing multiples from transactions involving comparable companies. Size and liquidity adjustments are typical refinements within the market approach. A P/E multiple applied to earnings is a market-based multiplier, distinct from the income approach, which discounts projected future cash flows.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Risk-adjusted discount rate must reflect market participant assumptions
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Silverline Corp. values an intangible asset using a discounted cash flow model. Management uses a 9% discount rate reflecting its own WACC. Market participant analysis indicates comparable intangible assets are discounted at 14% due to higher perceived risk. Projected cash flows are $300,000 per year for 5 years. Under ASC 820, which discount rate should Silverline use, and what is the approximate fair value?', '["9% (Silverline''s WACC), resulting in approximately $1,167,000", "The average of 9% and 14% (11.5%), resulting in approximately $1,097,000", "A risk-free rate of 3%, resulting in approximately $1,374,000", "14% (market participant rate), resulting in approximately $1,030,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 820-10-35-16A requires fair value to reflect assumptions market participants would use, not entity-specific assumptions. The discount rate must reflect the risk market participants assign to the cash flows. At 14%, the present value of $300,000 annually for 5 years is approximately $1,030,000. Using the entity''s own WACC of 9% ignores the market participant standard required by ASC 820.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Valuation process description required for Level 3
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Under ASC 820, which fair value hierarchy level requires disclosure of the valuation process, including how the entity develops unobservable inputs?', '["Level 3", "Level 1", "Level 2", "All levels equally"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 820-10-50-2(e) requires a description of the valuation process for Level 3 measurements, including how unobservable inputs are developed and the policies governing valuation. This helps users assess reliability of measurements with the greatest estimation uncertainty. Level 1 and Level 2 measurements rely on observable inputs and do not require a valuation process description.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Equity securities measurement alternative: observable price adjustment
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Oakmont Ventures holds an equity investment in a private company with no readily determinable fair value and elects the measurement alternative under ASC 321-10-35-2. During the year, an observable transaction in the investee''s stock occurs at a price indicating the investment is worth $150,000 more than Oakmont''s carrying amount. How should Oakmont account for this?', '["Ignore the observable transaction because the measurement alternative uses only impairment testing", "Adjust the carrying amount upward by $150,000 and recognize the change in net income", "Adjust the carrying amount upward by $150,000 and recognize the change in OCI", "Remeasure the entire investment at full fair value going forward, abandoning the measurement alternative"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 321-10-35-2 provides a measurement alternative: cost minus impairment, adjusted for observable price changes from orderly transactions in the same or similar securities. The $150,000 upward adjustment is recognized in net income, not OCI. OCI treatment applies to available-for-sale debt securities under ASC 326, not equity securities under the measurement alternative.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluating multiple instruments for fair value option eligibility
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Westfield Financial''s CFO evaluates four items for the fair value option under ASC 825: (1) a $5 million note receivable from a customer, (2) the company''s pension obligation with a projected benefit obligation of $12 million, (3) a firm commitment to purchase inventory under a fixed-price forward contract that is a financial instrument, and (4) the company''s interest in a consolidated subsidiary. How many of these items are eligible for the fair value option?', '["One item only", "All four items", "Two — the note receivable and the firm commitment", "Three — all except the pension obligation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 825-10-15-4 and 15-5 identify eligible and excluded items. The note receivable is an eligible recognized financial asset. The firm commitment involving a financial instrument is eligible per ASC 825-10-15-4(b). However, pension obligations (excluded per ASC 825-10-15-5(a)) and consolidated subsidiary interests (excluded per ASC 825-10-15-5(c)) are specifically excluded. Choosing three overlooks that consolidated subsidiaries are also excluded.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Fair value option: changes through net income (assets)
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Camden Corp. elects the fair value option under ASC 825 for a $3 million corporate bond investment. At year-end, the bond''s fair value increases to $3,180,000 due to declining market interest rates. Under ASC 825, how should Camden recognize the $180,000 fair value increase?', '["In accumulated other comprehensive income", "As a direct adjustment to retained earnings", "Deferred until the bond is sold or matures", "In net income as a gain on fair value adjustment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 825-10-35-1 requires changes in fair value of items measured under the fair value option to be reported in earnings. The $180,000 increase is a gain in net income. OCI treatment applies to AFS debt securities under the standard ASC 320 model, not to instruments where the fair value option was elected — ASC 825 routes all changes through net income (with the narrow exception of instrument-specific credit risk on liabilities).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Assets held for sale as nonrecurring fair value measurement
(2, 'Fair Value', 'Cascade Corp. classifies a manufacturing plant as held for sale under ASC 360-10-45 and writes it down from a carrying amount of $6.2 million to fair value less costs to sell of $5.5 million. Under ASC 820, this fair value measurement is classified as:', '["Nonrecurring, because the asset is written down to fair value only when carrying amount exceeds fair value less costs to sell", "Recurring, because the plant must be remeasured at fair value each quarter while held for sale", "Not subject to ASC 820 because held-for-sale assets have their own measurement guidance in ASC 360", "Recurring if the asset remains classified as held for sale for more than one reporting period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 820-10-50-1 classifies fair value measurements of assets held for sale as nonrecurring. These assets are measured at the lower of carrying amount or fair value less costs to sell, so fair value is relevant only when a write-down occurs — it is not a periodic remeasurement to fair value as defined by ASC 820. Ongoing monitoring does not constitute a recurring fair value measurement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — ASC 820 defines how to measure, not when to measure
(2, 'Fair Value', 'What is the primary role of ASC 820, Fair Value Measurement, in the GAAP framework?', '["It requires all assets and liabilities to be measured at fair value", "It defines how to measure fair value when other standards require or permit it", "It establishes which financial statements must be presented", "It supersedes all other measurement standards for financial instruments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 820-10-05-1 establishes that ASC 820 defines fair value, provides a measurement framework, and requires disclosures — but does not itself mandate fair value measurement. Other standards (ASC 320, ASC 350, ASC 805, ASC 815) determine when fair value applies. Stating that ASC 820 requires all items at fair value is incorrect because it only provides the measurement methodology when other standards call for it.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
